{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwTEcdqQCRST6LUnAsQ2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The dashboard and the rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. I haven't opened the dashboard to check the panels render, and the rule hasn't run yet (its status is \"pending\").\n\n**What the data holds:** `kibana_sample_data_logs` has one document per web request, with `timestamp` as the time field. The fields I used are:\n- `response`, the HTTP status code, stored as text. I used its `.keyword` version.\n- `bytes`, the response size.\n- `extension`, the file type requested.\n- `geo.*`, `machine.os`, `clientip`, `url` and `agent`, which I didn't chart.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-3b8ede6d Web logs overview\" (`eff-3b8ede6d-web-logs-overview-d5f3a8d4d6cd`) has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart.\n- Responses by code, as a pie chart.\n- Average bytes by file extension, as a bar chart.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-3b8ede6d 5xx spike\" (`238d2384-3160-431f-bf0a-38cd23288942`) is an Elasticsearch query rule that runs every minute. It counts documents in the last 5 minutes where `response.keyword` matches `5*`, and fires when the count is greater than 10. I didn't attach an action (such as a notification), so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana.\n\nThe sample data's timestamps are fixed when it's installed, so the rule may find no recent documents until fresh data arrives."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1255,"cache_read_input_tokens":19098,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1255},"output_tokens":518,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4eb1cb7-866c-5bbf-8d81-a8d5acd83be3","uuid